<p>Alissa Oakes successfully defended her PhD thesis, “The Complement Cascade in Myeloproliferative Neoplasms,” on June 6, 2024.<!-- excerpt end --></p>

<p>As a member of Dr. Patrycja Dubielecka-Szczerba’s laboratory at Brown University, Alissa studied blood cancer-initiating stem cells and their roles in the development, progression, and relapse of myeloproliferative neoplasms. Her work contributed to the lab’s translational goal of identifying ways to target neoplasm-initiating stem cells.</p>

<p>Following her successful defense, Alissa celebrated in Sidney Frank Hall with her advisor, lab mates, family, and members of the Brown community. Her colleagues marked the occasion with a playful lab-themed refreshment: cranberry and pineapple juice served in blood-donor bags. Alissa’s daughter attended both the defense and celebration, and family members traveled from across the United States to share the milestone.</p>

<p>Congratulations, Dr. Oakes!</p>

<p><a href="https://tsgp.brown.edu/news/2024-08-29/congratulations-dr-oakes">Read the original announcement from Brown University</a>.</p>

